<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/intermediate/maps-projections-and-gis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You already know how to read a map: scale bar, key, grid references, the north arrow.
    This page teaches the layer underneath — <strong>how maps are actually made, why every
    one of them distorts the world, and how modern Geographic Information Systems (GIS) turn
    stacks of map layers into answers to "where?" questions</strong>. The governing principle:
    maps are arguments. Every projection sacrifices some property of the globe to preserve
    another, and every dataset chosen for a GIS layer foregrounds a story. A cartographer who
    understands the mechanics can see the argument; one who does not is being argued at.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="projections, distortions and the birth of spatial analysis" widget="equationStep" config={projCfg} />

  <h2>Why every flat map must lie</h2>
  <p>
    A globe is the only true image of the Earth, and you cannot flatten a curved surface
    without stretching, tearing or squashing it — try flattening an orange peel. Map
    projections are systematic recipes for doing that flattening, and each recipe can
    preserve at most a few of the four properties you might want: <strong>shape</strong>
    (local angles), <strong>area</strong> (relative sizes), <strong>distance</strong>, and
    <strong>direction</strong>. No flat map preserves all four; most preserve one well and
    sacrifice the rest. So cartography's first rule: <em>choose the projection whose
    distortion hurts least for your purpose</em>. A navigator, who steers by compass bearing,
    wants direction; a demographer comparing countries wants area; an airline plotting the
    shortest route wants great circles (which is why flights to Asia arc near the pole on a
    flat map — the "curve" is the straight line, seen on a distorted sheet).
  </p>

  <h2>Projections: choosing your lie</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>Mercator projection</strong> (1569) preserves angles: any straight line on it
    is a constant compass bearing (a rhumb line), which made it revolutionary for sailing —
    draw a line from Lisbon to Havana and read the bearing off the map. The price is area
    inflation that grows towards the poles: local scale stretches in proportion to 1 divided
    by the cosine of latitude, so at 60° everything is drawn twice as long in both
    directions, and hence four times too big in area. Greenland (72°N) is drawn roughly the
    size of Africa, though Africa is about 14 times larger — 30.4 million km² against
    2.2 million. Because Mercator became the default classroom and web map, generations
    internalised a world in which Europe and North America loom over the global south.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> Mercator is "a racist map" that was designed to flatter
    Europe. Its inventor Gerardus Mercator built it for compass navigation, three centuries
    before the countries it allegedly flatters existed in their modern form; the inflation
    is a mathematical consequence of preserving bearings, not a political choice. (Feel the
    arithmetic: the local stretch is 1 divided by the cosine of latitude, so at 60°N a
    kilometre is drawn 2 km long — 4 km² of ink for 1 km² of ground, and worse at Greenland's
    72°N.) The
    legitimate criticism is that a navigation tool became a general-purpose world map — the
    fix is choosing the right projection, not discarding the wrong-shamed one.
    <strong>Equal-area projections</strong> (Gall-Peters, Mollweide, Albers) solve size at
    the cost of sheared shapes; <strong>compromise projections</strong> such as Robinson
    (1963) and the Winkel tripel — National Geographic's standard since 1998 — minimise total
    distortion without eliminating any. And for showing the poles, azimuthal projections
    centred on a point beat everything.
  </p>

  <h2>Scale, symbols and generalisation</h2>
  <p>
    Scale expresses how much reality one map unit represents: on a 1:50,000 map, 1 cm equals
    500 m. Notice the direction of the words: <strong>large scale</strong> (1:10,000) shows
    small areas in great detail; <strong>small scale</strong> (1:1,000,000) shows large areas
    in little detail — the fraction is literally bigger or smaller. <em>Common misconception:</em>
    "large scale" means a map of a large region; it is exactly backwards. Every map also
    generalises: at small scales, roads straighten, coastlines smooth, villages vanish —
    decisions that are invisible unless you look for them. Symbols carry arguments too.
    A choropleth map (areas shaded by value) looks objective, but its bins and colours
    decide what jumps out: five classes hides more than ten; red dramatises; a breakpoint
    at 0 exaggerates a divide. Cartographer Mark Monmonier's classic <em>How to Lie with
    Maps</em> (1991) catalogues the toolkit — which is also, usefully, the toolkit for
    lying <em>with maps deliberately</em> when the honest design serves the message.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the land: contours and relief</h2>
  <p>
    Height is shown on topographic maps by <strong>contour lines</strong> — lines joining
    points of equal elevation. The rules decode the landscape: contours close together mean
    steep ground, wide apart mean gentle; a "V" of contours pointing uphill marks a valley
    (rivers cut the V), while the same shape pointing downhill is a spur; closed rings mark
    hills, and a hachured ring marks a depression. From contours you can compute real
    quantities — an exam favourite. A worked example: if a route climbs from the 200 m
    contour to the 500 m contour over 3 km of ground, the average gradient is 300 m over
    3,000 m, or 1 in 10. Cross-sections — profile drawings of the land along a line — turn
    contour data into a picture of what a walker or a road engineer will actually face.
    Relief maps are where the "map as argument" point bites hardest: shade the slopes
    differently and a plateau reads as wilderness, or as building land.
    Practice the decode on any ordnance-survey extract and it becomes reflex — the
    difference between reading a map and thinking geographically.
  </p>

  <h2>Vector and raster: the two data types</h2>
  <p>
    Every layer in a GIS is stored one of two ways. <strong>Vector</strong> data records
    discrete features as points (wells, crimes), lines (roads, rivers) and polygons (parcels,
    lakes), each with attribute tables — precise, compact, ideal for networks and boundaries.
    <strong>Raster</strong> data divides space into a grid of cells, each holding a value —
    elevation, temperature, land cover — continuous by nature and ideal for modelling
    (flood-water depth across a valley, pollutant dispersal). Choosing is a real design
    decision: a road network is vector logic (which junction connects to which); a landslide
    susceptibility model is raster logic (slope and soil and rain per cell, multiplied and
    summed). Analyses often convert between the two, and each conversion trades precision
    for convenience. Boundary and network data is almost always vector; continuous
    environmental surfaces are almost always raster — a good analyst can say in one
    sentence which a new dataset is, and why.
  </p>

  <h2>Coordinates, datums and the discipline of "where"</h2>
  <p>
    To store location, geography uses coordinate systems. Latitude and longitude are angles
    on a sphere (the Equator 0°, poles 90°N and 90°S); the Universal Transverse Mercator
    (UTM) system instead divides the world into 60 vertical zones and gives metre-precise
    eastings and northings, which surveyors and soldiers prefer. The subtle trap is the
    <strong>datum</strong> — the mathematical model of Earth's shape that coordinates are
    measured against. North American data stored in the old NAD27 datum read in a modern
    system can land 100 m or more from the truth, which for a pipeline or a landing strip is
    an expensive category of error. GPS satellites now give any phone metre-level position
    from a constellation of over thirty satellites (centimetres with survey corrections), so
    the practical skill of the modern geographer is less "finding position" than
    <em>knowing which coordinate system your data was born in</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>GIS: layers, queries and the founding detective story</h2>
  <p>
    A Geographic Information System is software that stacks spatial data — roads, population,
    elevation, rainfall, land ownership — as layers registered to the same coordinates, then
    answers questions by combining them. A spatial query reads like a sentence:
    <em>show all neighbourhoods within 5 km of a clinic, that flooded in the last decade,
    where population density exceeds 2,000 per km²</em>. Each clause is a layer operation;
    the answer is the overlap. The founding case study predates the software by a century:
    in the 1854 Broad Street cholera outbreak, physician John Snow plotted each death as a
    bar on a street map of Soho, London. The clustering pointed at the Broad Street water
    pump; Snow had the handle removed and the epidemic faded — roughly 600 deaths in the
    fortnight at its height. He strengthened the case statistically, comparing death rates
    of customers of two water companies drawing from upstream and downstream of a sewage
    outfall, but the map persuaded where tables had not. Modern public health still works
    this way: vaccination campaigns are planned by overlaying case clusters on road networks,
    and a supermarket chain "simply" runs Snow's logic on footfall.
  </p>
  <p>
    Real GIS projects combine simple operations into a weighted model. To site a wind farm,
    for instance: buffer all settlements by 800 m (exclude), buffer roads and rivers,
    mask out slopes steeper than 15° from a raster DEM (digital elevation model), keep areas
    with mean wind speed above 7 m/s, and score the remainder for grid-connection distance —
    the map of candidate sites falls out of arithmetic that every stakeholder can audit.
    The professional skill is less the software than decomposing a question into layer
    operations, and being explicit about each threshold.
  </p>
  <p>
    GIS layers are fed by <strong>remote sensing</strong> — satellite imagery (Landsat has
    imaged Earth continuously since 1972, the longest-running Earth record), radar that
    sees through cloud, and airborne LiDAR that strips forests away to reveal the ground
    beneath. Deforestation, glacier retreat, urban sprawl and flood extents are now
    <em>measured</em>, not estimated, and change is detectable year by year by differencing
    two images.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The intellectual spine of the field is <strong>Tobler's first law of geography</strong>
    (1970): "everything is related to everything else, but near things are more related than
    distant things." It sounds trivial and is not — it is the assumption that makes spatial
    statistics possible, because if location carried no information, a map would be a random
    scatter. Spatial autocorrelation statistics (Moran's I) test whether a pattern is
    clustered, random or dispersed; hot-spot analysis asks whether a cluster of disease
    cases, crimes or customers is real or just population density wearing a costume —
    always compare rates against the underlying population before declaring a cluster. The
    Mercator stretch has a modern sequel: Google Maps and its rivals use Web Mercator,
    which additionally treats the Earth as a sphere rather than an ellipsoid, sacrificing
    yet more accuracy for fast tile rendering — the same 1569 compromise, reborn for
    zoomable slippy maps. And the frontier questions are live ones: citizen-built
    OpenStreetMap (founded 2004) now rivals state mapping in many cities; location data from
    phones raises privacy questions no cartographer of 1970 imagined; and deep-learning
    models now read satellite imagery to count buildings and predict crop yields. The
    discipline Snow started with a pen and a street plan has become one of the most powerful
    lenses of the data age — and its first lesson stands: look at the projection, ask who
    drew the layers, and remember the map is an argument.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Monmonier, How to Lie with Maps; Johnson, The Ghost Map (Snow, 1854); Esri & QGIS documentation; Tobler (1970), “A Computer Movie Simulating Urban Growth”.</p>
